 "caveats": [
  "Saturation: MATH Level 5 frontier reached 94.7% (o1, Dec 2024) and 98.1% (GPT-5, Aug 2025); Epoch picked Level 5 originally because full MATH was close to saturated. OTIS Mock AIME reached 100% (gpt-5.5-pro pre-release, Apr 2026). FrontierMath Tier 4 v2 reached 100% (gpt-6.1-sol, Sept 29, 2026). Points at or above 95% carry saturated: true.",
  "Contamination: MATH (problems and worked solutions) has been public since March 2021, and MATH-style competition problems are abundant online; any model trained after 2021 may have seen the test problems or close variants. Epoch's MATH Level 5 page does not address contamination. OTIS Mock AIME 2024-2025 is three unofficial mock exams by Evan Chen: the 2024 exam was published Dec 22, 2023 and the 2025 I and II exams on Dec 20, 2024 (exam PDFs on web.evanchen.cc), so models released before Dec 22, 2023 cannot have seen any problem, models released before Dec 20, 2024 cannot have seen 30 of the 45, and later models could have seen all of them. FrontierMath is private (only 12 of 338 problems public) but was developed with OpenAI funding and OpenAI \"has exclusive access to a subset of the benchmark\" (Epoch). FrontierMath-Erdos statements are public on GitHub; Epoch says no proofs were known as of Aug 2026.",
  "FrontierMath v1 had errors in 42% of problems; v2 (June 12, 2026) corrected 123 Tier 1-3 problems and 12 Tier 4 problems and removed 12. Old v1 scores are kept only as other_series and should not be joined to v2.",
  "Everything here is auto-graded (answer matching or Lean proof checking): a verifiable domain. Epoch's scorers include a model-graded equivalence check for MATH (gemini-1.5-flash-002); before Sept 2025 the OTIS scorer sometimes graded blank answers as correct (Epoch).",
  "Dates are model release dates (Epoch's \"Release date\"); many older models were evaluated by Epoch long after release (see evaluated_at), which is fine for capability-by-release-date but means the curve was not observable in real time.",
  "Paper-reported 2021-2022 numbers use different prompting (fine-tuning, few-shot, majority voting) from Epoch's single-attempt runs; they are context, not a continuous series.",
  "Release events: the Sept 12, 2024 jump (o1-mini: MATH Level 5 53% to 89%, OTIS 10% to 47%) coincides with the first release of OpenAI's privately developed reasoning-RL models and is flagged release_event: true. The Oct 6, 2026 release of 722 manuscripts (events.json) is a release event with no benchmark score attached."
 ],
